Script details:

Android boot header v0 (little-endian):
- magic "ANDROID!"
- kernel_size, kernel_addr, ramdisk_size, ramdisk_addr, second_size, second_addr, tags_addr, page_size, header_version (or dt_size), os_version, name[16], cmdline[512], id[8], extra_cmdline[1024]
Specific layout:
struct boot_img_hdr_v0:
  uint8_t magic[8];
  uint32_t kernel_size;
  uint32_t kernel_addr;
  uint32_t ramdisk_size;
  uint32_t ramdisk_addr;
  uint32_t second_size;
  uint32_t second_addr;
  uint32_t tags_addr;
  uint32_t page_size;
  uint32_t header_version; // in v0 this was dt_size
  uint32_t os_version;
  uint8_t name[16];
  uint8_t cmdline[512];
  uint8_t id[8];
  uint8_t extra_cmdline[1024];
Total 1632 bytes.

If header_version field == 0 => dt_size = that field (in v0). Actually in boot header v0, the field at offset 40 is "dt_size" (for QCOM). In v1/v2 it's header_version. Distinguish: if value is 0 or 1 or 2 → header_version, else dt_size? Standard heuristic: if the value at offset 40 > 2, treat as dt_size and header_version=0. Samsung may use it as dt_size (qcom device). Some devices put dt_size there. If header_version>=1, additionally: recovery_dtbo_size, recovery_dtbo_offset, header_size, dtb_size, dtb_addr.

Samsung boot images sometimes are "SEANDROIDENFORCE" appended, and kernel may be a "zImage" (self-extracting) or raw Image.gz.

I'll write generic: read header, compute pages, dump sections. Also scan for gzip/xz/lz4/zstd magic at kernel start and decompress.

Then config extraction: search decompressed kernel for b"IKCFG_ST" ... find b"IKCFG_ED"; the gzip starts right after IKCFG_ST (8 bytes). Try gzip decompress of slice.

Also vmlinux extraction: if kernel is a self-extracting zImage (ARM32), the compressed payload (gzip/xz/lz4) is at an offset; but vmlinux extraction can be done via scripts/extract-vmlinux from upstream source (shell script works for many). We'll use upstream scripts/extract-vmlinux after downloading.

